{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787124031,"nonce":"d9AA7F5rII+D8ypS+GczIUB0mKI=","title":"**What is wrong.** Nothing ends a conversation once `alert_groups` is deleted, and nothing is designed to. The owner ruled on 2026-08-19 that a conversation holds exactly ONE Case and that a conversation is **not immortal**: after a threshold, the next fact should BROADCAST or start a NEW message rather than append to the thread — and the threshold should be operator-configurable. That mechanism does not exist. This ticket is the scope `7570090` never had.","message":"**The demonstration.** Today the rollover is an accident of row identity, not a decision. A generation is its own `alert_groups` row with its own UUID — `groups_key_gen_uniq UNIQUE (org_id, group_key, generation)` (`db/migrations/00008_grouping.sql:46`) — and `channel_threads.subject_id` holds that per-generation id (`00011:155`, inline comment `alert_groups.id (one generation)`). So a re-opened group gets a new row, a new UUID, and therefore a new thread **by ordinary uniqueness**, with no code deciding anything.\n\n⛔ `7570090` REMOVES THAT AND DOES NOT REPLACE IT. Its own answer is *\"`channel_threads` gains `generation`\"*, justified by channel archival — *\"a per-channel generation is MORE honest than a shared one, because a Slack channel can be archived while Teams lives\"* — and **it never says what ADVANCES the number.** A grep for `generation + 1` returns nothing anywhere in the tree; nothing increments it today because nothing needs to. Add the column without a trigger and it is a constant.\n\n⚠️ AND THE TICKET'S STATED PREMISE FOR IT IS FALSE, which is why this cannot be read out of that ticket. It claims today's generation is *\"smuggled in through `alert_groups.generation` inside `subject_id`\"*. Nothing is packed into or decoded out of that UUID — it is a plain primary key (`00008:15`). Verified twice, independently, during the run that filed this.\n\n**Why it matters.** Under the ruling, a conversation is keyed to a Case. Absent a rollover rule the thread lives as long as its key does, and two things then break quietly rather than loudly:\n\n- **The reminder latch**, if it survives. A latch keyed to a stable conversation fires once and suppresses every LATER incident in it — a silent MISS. (`bd0fb1d` removes the feature, which dissolves this; filed separately so neither ticket depends on the other landing first.)\n- **`frozen`** (`e5c060b`) meant \"the group closed\". With one Case per conversation and no generation, the state has no trigger left. The owner ruled it dropped — so the thread's lifecycle currently has no terminal state at all, by decision, and the rollover rule is what replaces it.\n\nThere is also a plain product cost: an incident in March replying into a thread from January is worse than a new thread, and nothing in the system currently prefers the second.\n\n**Where.** `db/migrations/00008_grouping.sql:5-7,21,46` (what a generation is and what makes one) · `db/migrations/00011_notification.sql:155,168` (`subject_id`, `threads_subject_uniq`) · `internal/notification/repository/threads.go:97-107` (`ensureThreadSQL`'s `ON CONFLICT` — the uniqueness constraint IS the idempotency arbiter, so the rollover key must be part of it or it cannot roll) · `internal/notification/service/notify.go` (`threadSubjectOf`, now reading `(conversation_kind, conversation_id)` off the row since `0e66594`) · `internal/identity/domain/org.go:222-226` (`DefaultGroupCloseDelay`, pinned equal to `DefaultRefireGrace`) · `docs/design/SPEC.md` §H.9 and the §D.1 tuning rows.\n\n⭐ `group_close_delay_s` ALREADY EXISTS AND ALREADY MEANS THE RIGHT THING — \"this generation has been quiet long enough to close\". It is the obvious candidate for the idle half of the threshold, and reusing it keeps ONE number governing one concept instead of minting a second knob an operator has to reconcile with the first. ⚠️ But it is pinned at or above `DefaultRefireGrace` by `TestGroupCloseDelayDoesNotDefeatTheRefireGrace`, and ADR 0040 §6 explains why — so repurposing it is a decision about that pin, not a free reuse.\n\n**Scope.** medium, and it carries a design decision that is the owner's rather than the implementer's: which axis the threshold is on. Idle time, reply count, both, or elapsed wall-clock are four different products. A reply cap is the one Slack itself pushes back on; an idle timer is the one an operator can reason about from their own `group_interval`.\n\n**Done when.** A conversation has a stated, configurable end. On crossing it, the next fact either opens a NEW conversation or broadcasts, deliberately — and which of the two is a decision recorded here rather than discovered from the code. The threshold is bounded, defaulted, and reachable from the settings contract like every other tuning knob, with `guide` copy that does the arithmetic against the operator's own Alertmanager the way the surviving knobs do. Whatever key makes a new conversation is part of `threads_subject_uniq`, or `ensureThreadSQL`'s `ON CONFLICT` will keep resolving to the old thread and the rollover will silently no-op. A test asserts the rollover BOTH ways: that a fact inside the threshold appends, and that one past it does not. And SPEC records that a conversation has no terminal state other than this one, since `frozen` was withdrawn (`e5c060b`) and the generation that used to end it is gone.","files":null}]}